 ## The gitleaks program
 
-Gitleaks is a program that scans files and history for leaked secrets. A few tests (`tests/test_hygiene.py`, `tests/test_gitleaks_exceptions.py`, `tests/test_gitleaks_semgrep_lock.py`) run it. When the variable `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN` is not set, a test that cannot find the program is skipped, and the skip reason says that no gitleaks program was found (`python -m pytest -q -rs` prints it). When `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN` is set to any non-empty value, a missing program makes those tests fail with "gitleaks binary not found: put it on PATH or set GITLEAKS_BIN". So you need the program only if you set `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN`.
+Gitleaks is a program that scans files and history for leaked secrets. A few tests (`tests/test_hygiene.py`, `tests/test_gitleaks_exceptions.py`, `tests/test_gitleaks_semgrep_lock.py`) run it. When the environment variable `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN` is not set, a test that cannot find the program is skipped. The skip reason reads "no gitleaks program on PATH (set GITLEAKS_BIN); set DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN=1 to make this a failure" (`python -m pytest -q -rs` prints it). When `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN` is set to any non-empty value, a missing program makes those tests fail with "gitleaks binary not found: put it on PATH or set GITLEAKS_BIN" (the word binary in that message means the program). So you need the program only if you set `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN`. With `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN` set, one test also scans the git history, so it needs a full clone, not a shallow one. If `GITLEAKS_BIN` names a path that does not exist, the tests stop with an error that names `GITLEAKS_BIN`, with or without `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN`.
 
-The version this repository was checked with is 8.30.1 (the version in `.github/workflows/lint.yml`). To set it up on Linux:
+The version this repository was checked with is 8.30.1 (the version in `.github/workflows/lint.yml`). The steps below are for Linux on a 64-bit Intel or AMD computer (x86_64); an ARM computer needs a different archive. To set it up:
 
-1. Open the gitleaks releases page of its GitHub project and download two files for version 8.30.1: the Linux x64 archive and the checksums file of that release.
-2. In the folder that holds both files, run `sha256sum -c` on the checksums file (for example `sha256sum -c --ignore-missing <the checksums file>`). It must print OK for the archive. If it does not, delete the archive and download it again.
-3. Unpack the archive into a folder of your own.
-4. Tell the tests where the program is: set `GITLEAKS_BIN` to the path of the program (`export GITLEAKS_BIN=<folder>/gitleaks`), or put the folder on `PATH`.
+1. Find the releases page of the gitleaks project on its public code-hosting site (search the web for "gitleaks releases"), open version 8.30.1 and download two files: `gitleaks_8.30.1_linux_x64.tar.gz` and `gitleaks_8.30.1_checksums.txt`. The checksums file is a text file that lists the expected SHA-256 fingerprint of each download.
+2. In the folder that holds both files, run `sha256sum -c --ignore-missing gitleaks_8.30.1_checksums.txt`. It recomputes the fingerprint of the archive and compares it; it must print `gitleaks_8.30.1_linux_x64.tar.gz: OK`. If it does not, delete the archive and download it again. This check finds a damaged download; it does not prove who made the file, because the checksums file comes from the same place.
+3. Unpack the archive into a folder of your own: `mkdir -p <folder> && tar -xzf gitleaks_8.30.1_linux_x64.tar.gz -C <folder>`.
+4. Check the program: `<folder>/gitleaks version` must print 8.30.1.
+5. Tell the tests where the program is: set `GITLEAKS_BIN` to the path of the program (`export GITLEAKS_BIN=<folder>/gitleaks`), or put the folder on `PATH`.
 
-Then run `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN=1 python -m pytest -q`.
+Then, from the repository root with `.venv` switched on and the development lock installed (see "How to run the tests"), run `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN=1 python -m pytest -q`.
 
